course: everyday
lesson: 230
title: Hiking and camping safely
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A good hike is planned: water, snacks, a map, a torch and a plan to be back before dark. Small problems like thirst, tiredness or a rubbing boot should be told to your grown-up early, before they become big ones.
explain: The biggest dangers on a walk are edges and water: cliff edges can crumble, quarries hide deep cold water, and flooded streams are stronger than they look. When the way is blocked, the safe choice is to turn back or find a way round.
explain: Camping means living outdoors for a while, so you choose a safe place for the tent, keep the camp clean, and leave no litter behind.
explain: Example: a family in British Columbia camps in bear country. They keep all food in a bear-proof container away from the tents, never take snacks into the tent, and sleep safely.
fact: When you are tired and thirsty on a walk, tell your grown-up, rest and drink water, because exhaustion leads to falls and heat sickness.
fact: Tell a grown-up early about a blister and cover it with a plaster or blister pad before it becomes an open sore.
fact: Stay well back from cliff edges and behind any fence, because edges can crumble.
fact: In a field with cows, walk calmly round the edge, close gates and keep dogs on a lead, because cows protect their calves.
fact: If a fallen tree or flooded stream blocks the path, turn back or find a safe way round with your grown-up.
fact: On a narrow path, step aside and say hello; walkers going uphill usually have the right of way.
fact: Check trail markers at every junction so you stay on the right path.
fact: Carry a torch and plan to be back before dark, because paths are hard to follow in the dark.
fact: Keep away from quarries and old mines, because rocks fall and quarry water is very deep and cold.
fact: Pitch a tent on flat, high ground away from dead trees, rivers and the bottom of slopes.
fact: At a campsite without toilets, go with a grown-up at least 60 metres (about 200 feet) from water and bury it or pack it out.
fact: In bear country, store food in bear-proof containers and keep a clean camp, because bears follow food smells.
fact: Never use a stove, heater or barbecue inside a tent, because of fire and carbon monoxide, a poison gas you cannot smell.
fact: Leave a campsite as you found it, and walk carefully between tents because ropes and pegs trip people up.
exercise: You feel very tired and thirsty halfway up a hill. What should you do? | Tell your grown-up, rest and drink water | Keep going quietly so you are not a nuisance | Run the rest of the way to finish faster | Eat salty crisps instead of drinking
exercise: Your heel starts to rub in your boot. What is best? | Tell a grown-up early and cover it with a plaster | Ignore it until the end of the walk, because rubs always stop on their own | Take your boots off and walk the rest of the way barefoot | Pop it with a stick
exercise: There is an amazing view from a cliff edge. Where should you stand? | Well back from the edge, behind any fence | Right at the very edge to get the best photo of the view | On top of the fence so you can see over it | On a flat rock that sticks right out over the edge
exercise: Your path crosses a field with cows and calves. What should you do? | Walk calmly round the edge and close the gate | Run straight through the middle to get across fast | Go over and stroke the calves while their mothers watch | Leave the gate open for the next walkers
exercise: A stream across your path is flooded after rain. What should you do? | Turn back or find a safe way round with your grown-up | Wade straight through together, holding hands tightly | Jump from rock to rock to get across without getting wet | Swim across quickly
exercise: You meet walkers coming uphill on a narrow path. What is polite? | Step aside, say hello and let them pass | Make them step right off the path so you can pass | Walk straight on, since you were there first | Turn your back on them and say nothing at all
exercise: You come to a fork in the trail. What should you do? | Check the trail marker before choosing | Take whichever path looks shorter and flatter | Always turn left, because that is the rule on trails | Split up so you can try both paths at once
exercise: The sun is going down and home is still far away. What helps most? | Tell your grown-up and use a torch | Run on ahead of everyone in the dark | Leave the path to take a quick shortcut home | Sit down and wait for the moon to rise
exercise: There is an old quarry with a pool of clear water. What should you do? | Stay away and never swim there | Swim, because clear water is safe | Climb the quarry walls for fun | Jump in from the edge
exercise: Where is the best place to pitch your tent? | On flat, high ground away from dead trees and rivers | At the bottom of a slope, where it is out of the wind | Right next to the river so water is close for cooking | Under a big dead tree for shade
exercise: Why should a tent not be pitched in a dip at the bottom of a slope? | Rainwater collects there and can flood it | Bears always walk through dips | Dips are too sunny in the morning | The ground is too soft for pegs
exercise: You need the toilet at a camp with no toilets. What should you do? | Go with a grown-up well away from water and bury it | Go right next to the stream so the water washes it away | Go behind the nearest tent | Hold it until you get home, even if the trip lasts three days
exercise: You are camping in bear country. Where should food be kept? | In a bear-proof container away from the tents | Inside your tent, so that you can guard it all night | In the bottom of your sleeping bag to keep it warm | Hung on your tent door
exercise: It is cold, and someone suggests lighting the camping stove inside the tent. Why is that dangerous? | It can start a fire and make carbon monoxide gas | The tent will get too warm for anyone to sleep in | The smell of cooked food will stay in the tent for weeks | Stoves stop working when inside
exercise: What should you do with litter when packing up camp? | Pick it all up and take it with you | Bury it under the ashes of the fire | Leave it in a neat pile for the park rangers | Throw it into the bushes
exercise: Why should you not run between tents at night? | Tent ropes and pegs can trip you | It wakes up the bears | The tents might fall on you | Running at night is illegal
test: A boy in Arizona on a hot hike feels dizzy and stops sweating. What is wise? | Tell his grown-up, rest in the shade and drink | Keep going so that he is not left behind by the group | Sprint to the top before it gets hotter | Wait until the end of the hike before saying anything at all
test: A girl in the Lake District wants to climb over a fence to see a waterfall from the cliff top. Why not? | The edge can crumble and a fall could kill | The waterfall always looks better from far away | Fences like that are only meant for farm animals | She might get her coat dirty on the wet fence
test: A family reaches a giant fallen tree across the trail. Which choice is safest? | Find a safe way round or turn back | Climb over the slippery trunk one by one | Crawl underneath the trunk on your tummy | Push the tree off the path together
test: A walker leaves a farm gate open behind him. What could happen? | Animals could escape onto the road | The farmer will be pleased to have fresh air in | The cows will stay calmer with more room | Nothing, because farm gates are only there for show
test: A camper says «I can't smell anything, so the heater in the tent is safe». Why is he wrong? | Carbon monoxide is a poison gas with no smell | Heaters only ever give off smoke that you can see | Tents let all gases out at once | Carbon monoxide always smells strongly of rotten eggs
test: Two families arrive at a campsite. One picks a spot next to a river at the foot of a hill. What is the risk? | The low ground by the river could flood in rain | The river will be far too noisy for anyone to sleep | They will get too much sun | Nothing, because rivers keep campers safe from animals
test: A boy keeps a chocolate bar in his tent in a Canadian national park. Why is that a bad idea? | Bears are drawn by food smells | It will melt overnight | Chocolate attracts mosquitoes | Rangers will take it away
test: At the end of the trip, a girl finds bottle caps in the grass that are not hers. What should she do? | Pick them up too and leave the place clean | Leave them there, as they are not hers to touch | Bury them so nobody sees | Throw them far out into the lake where nobody will see them
test: A blister on a girl's toe is small at the start of a long walk. Why should she deal with it now? | A small rub left alone becomes a painful open sore | Blisters always disappear if you just keep walking more | It is only worth fixing at the end | Plasters stop working after an hour, so it is better to wait
test: on the walk my heel's rubbing, it's fine i'll keep going | Tell your grown-up now and cover it before it's a sore | Ignore it till you get home, rubs always go away by themselves | Take off your boots and walk barefoot | Run so the walk ends sooner n your feet get a rest
test: cows in the field w calves, can i go say hi | No, walk calmly round the edge and leave them | Yes, cows love being patted, calves most of all | Yes, if you run in quickly | Only if you bring the dog along to keep them back
test: freezing in the tent, can we bring the little stove in to warm up | No, never, fire and poison gas danger | Yes, if you leave the door open a bit | Yes, just for ten minutes before bed | Only if it's a small stove n u watch it
test: we're camping in bear country, can i keep my sweets in the tent | No, store food in a bear-proof container | Yes, sweets in wrappers don't smell at all | Yes, under your pillow is the safest place | Only if they're wrapped up tight in a bag
explain: A family in Arizona went hiking at midday in summer with one small bottle of water; the boy got heat exhaustion and they had to call rescue.
explain: A girl in the Lake District went off alone to explore near camp; the fog came down, she got lost and the mountain rescue team searched for her through the night.
fact: Heat and too little water can make hikers very ill quickly, and every year rescuers are called to people who ran out of water.
fact: Fog and dark can make even a short walk confusing, so hikers stay together and tell someone their route.
test: one bottle of water enough for the big hike? | No, take plenty and drink often | Yes, just drink it at the end | Yes, if you go fast | Only in winter when it's cold
test: Why should Ella not go off alone from camp? | She could get lost, especially in fog or dark | Camps are boring when you are on your own | She will miss dinner | Her tent could blow away
test: feeling dizzy and headachy on the hot trail | Stop in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Keep going to finish, it'll pass when u get there | Run to the car | Lie down in the sun for a bit n have a nap
test: did u hear a family put a little heater in their tent cos it was cold and they all got sick from gas and had to be rescued? real lesson? | Never use a heater or stove inside a tent | Use a heater but always leave a small gap in the door | Only use it for a few minutes | Electric blankets are just as bad, so never use anything warm
test: apparently some hikers ran out of water on a trail and one fainted and they needed a helicopter. what's the true lesson? | Carry enough water and tell an adult when thirsty | Hikers never need water on short walks | Drink only at the end of the walk so you don't need a wee | Helicopters rescue everyone, so there's no need to worry
test: my mate swears his uncle's cow chased him across a whole field and trampled his dog. probably true lesson? | Cows protect calves; walk calmly round the edge, dog on lead | Cows are harmless, so just walk straight through them | Run through the field as fast as you can | Let the dog off its lead so it can scare the cows away
exam: Identify the principal hazard of using a stove or heater inside a tent. | Fire and carbon monoxide, a poison gas without smell | The tent becomes too bright for its occupants to sleep | The food is cooked too quickly | The smoke attracts large numbers of biting insects
exam: Which of the following constitutes the proper siting of a tent? | Flat high ground, away from dead trees, rivers and slope bottoms | Directly beside a river, so as to ensure a supply of water | Beneath a dead tree, for shelter | At the bottom of a steep slope, where the wind cannot reach it
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of cliff edges. | One remains well back and behind any fence | One stands at the very edge in order to view the scene | One climbs over fences to see further | One leans out over the edge whilst holding a friend
exam: Ascertain the reason for which food is stored in bear-proof containers in bear country. | Bears follow the smells of food | Bears fear plastic boxes | Food keeps colder inside them | It is required only in winter
